patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ source: null, position: 0, audible: false, oneShot: false, prevPlaying: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const key = `${ctx.componentPath}/${ctx.id}#${ctx.loopIndex}`;
const ref = playable(ctx.input("sound"));   // AssetRef; null when empty, a live handle, or a missing asset
if (!sameRef(ref, state.source)) {
  stopVoice(key);
  Object.assign(state, { source: ref, position: 0, audible: false, oneShot: false });
}
const d = soundDuration(ref);                // seconds; 0 while unknown
const loop = ctx.input("loop");
const rate = clamp(finiteOr(ctx.input("rate"), 1), 0.03, 32);
let finished = false;

// 1. Advance by the time since the previous frame.
if (state.audible) {
  state.position += ctx.dt * rate;
  if (d > 0 && state.position >= d) {
    finished = true;
    if (loop) state.position %= d;
    else { state.position = d; state.oneShot = false; }
  }
}

// 2. Commands.
const playing = ctx.input("playing");
const rose = playing && !state.prevPlaying;
if (!playing && state.prevPlaying) state.oneShot = false;
state.prevPlaying = playing;
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) { state.position = 0; state.oneShot = false; }
else if (ctx.pulsed("play")) { state.position = 0; state.oneShot = true; }
else if (rose && d > 0 && state.position >= d) state.position = 0;

// 3. Whether sound comes out on this frame.
const atEnd = d > 0 && !loop && state.position >= d;
state.audible = ref !== null && (playing || state.oneShot) && !atEnd;
if (state.audible) ctx.requestNextFrame();
syncVoice(key, state);                       // platform side effects, below

ctx.output("currentTime", state.position);
ctx.output("duration", d);
ctx.output("progress", d > 0 ? Math.min(state.position / d, 1) : 0);
ctx.output("isPlaying", state.audible);
ctx.output("metering", ref === null ? null : liveHandle(key));
if (finished) ctx.pulse("finished");
```

- Pulses: `play` and `reset`. Same-frame precedence: `reset` > `play`. Reset never starts anything, but while Playing is on the sound keeps playing from the start. Play while a sound is running restarts it; pulses on consecutive frames each restart.
- Playing: a level input. Its rising edge resumes from the current position, or from 0 when the sound had finished. Its falling edge pauses, keeps the position, and cancels a one-shot.
- Timing: the playhead advances by `dt × rate` starting the frame after a start, so the start frame reads 0 and results match at 60 and 120 fps. Finished fires on the frame whose advance reaches Duration.
- Frame 0: position 0. With Playing on, or a Play pulse on frame 0, Is Playing is true on frame 0.
- Retargeting: a new Sound stops the old voice and starts over at 0, following the current Playing state (a finished one-shot doesn't replay). Changing Loop, Volume, Rate, Pitch, or Pan applies live without seeking or restarting. Turning Loop off mid-sound plays to the end, then stops.
- Edge cases: an empty Sound or a missing asset is silent, outputs 0 and false, and ignores Play. A live handle (any Metering output) isn't playable and counts as empty, with one warning per restart. Non-finite Volume, Rate, Pitch, or Pan use 1, 1, 0, and 0 with one warning; otherwise they clamp to [0, 1], [0.03, 32], [−2400, 2400], and [−1, 1]. While Duration is unknown the playhead advances but never finishes or loops, and Progress is 0. A load failure warns once per source and behaves as empty.
- Loops: one independent voice per loop index, so a loop of sounds or of Play pulses plays several at once. New indices start stopped; removed indices stop their voice. At most 32 voices sound at a time; later indices stay silent (their outputs still advance) with one warning.
- Muted: every voice stops; outputs are 0, false, and null; no pulses.
- Platform and simulation: side effects go through `ctx.services.platform.audio`, keyed as above, at most once per frame per index: `play` when `audible` rises (from `state.position`), `pause` when it falls, `seek(0)` on Reset, Play, or replay, `update` when Loop, Volume, Rate, Pitch, or Pan change (`ctx.changed`), and `stop` on a new source. Once the platform has loaded the voice, its clock is the truth: each frame `state.position` comes from `state(key).currentTime` and Duration from `state(key).duration`, and Finished fires when its loop count increases or `ended` rises. While the voice loads, or the browser's autoplay rule blocks it, the position holds and Is Playing is false; playback begins on the first allowed frame if still wanted. Web hosts use Web Audio: a time-stretching AudioWorklet so Rate keeps pitch and Pitch keeps speed (pitch factor `2^(cents / 1200)`), then a `StereoPannerNode` and a `GainNode`. Headless simulation and automated runs have no platform audio: the simulated playhead runs deterministically, Duration comes from the asset record, and the patch logs `soundPlayer: audio is silent in simulation` once per restart.
- Platform contract: `platform.audio` keyed voices: `play(key, source, { from, loop, volume, rate, pitch, pan })`, `pause`, `seek`, `update`, `stop`, and `state(key): { status, currentTime, duration, ended, loops }`. Pitch goes to the host in semitones (cents / 100). Until a voice has loaded, Duration comes from `services.mediaInfo`. The Metering output is `{ live: "audio/<key>" }`.
- Restart and dispose: stop every voice, reset state, and release decoded buffers.
- Coming from Origami: Sound Player Settings (`origami.soundplayer.settings`) is folded in as advanced inputs. Origami replays a finished sound with Reset; Sonobe also replays on a Playing rising edge at the end, and adds the Play pulse.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const url = ctx.input("url").trim();
if (url !== "") {
  ctx.output("output", isLoadableUrl(url, "image") ? { url } : null);   // http:, https:, data:image/…, blob:
} else {
  const ref = ctx.input("image");                                       // { assetId } | { url } | null
  const exists = ref !== null && (ref.url !== undefined || ctx.services.resolveAssetUrl(ref.assetId) !== undefined);
  ctx.output("output", exists ? ref : null);
}
```

- Encoding: in a document, Image holds `{ "asset": "<assetId>" }`; at runtime it's `{ assetId }`. The output is the same reference, not decoded pixels. Layers and media patches load it.
- Frame 0: outputs the reference immediately. Loading is the consumer's job (the Image layer's `loading` output, Image Info's `loading`).
- Retargeting: a new asset or URL replaces the output on the same frame.
- Edge cases: an asset id missing from the registry outputs null (core raises the missing-asset diagnostic). A URL with any other scheme (`file:`, `javascript:`, a relative path) outputs null with one warning per restart. A whitespace-only URL counts as empty. A URL that fails to load still outputs its reference; consumers report the failure.
- Loops: per index; a loop of URLs gives a loop of pictures, one Image layer instance each.
- Platform and simulation: web pictures from other sites display without CORS, but patches that read pixels (detection, Snapshot in the web player) need CORS headers. Headless simulation outputs the same references without loading anything.
- Coming from Origami: Origami stores the picture inside the patch. Importers put the embedded resource into the asset registry and set Image to that asset.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const url = ctx.input("url").trim();
if (url !== "") {
  ctx.output("output", isLoadableUrl(url, "video") ? { url } : null);   // http:, https:, data:video/…, blob:
} else {
  const ref = ctx.input("video");                                       // { assetId } | { url } | null
  const exists = ref !== null && (ref.url !== undefined || ctx.services.resolveAssetUrl(ref.assetId) !== undefined);
  ctx.output("output", exists ? ref : null);
}
```

- Encoding: in a document, Video holds `{ "asset": "<assetId>" }`; at runtime it's `{ assetId }`. The output is the same reference; the Video layer loads and plays it.
- Frame 0: outputs the reference immediately.
- Retargeting: a new asset or URL replaces the output on the same frame; the Video layer starts the new clip according to its own Play, Loop, and Scrub properties.
- Edge cases: an asset id missing from the registry outputs null (core raises the missing-asset diagnostic). A URL with any other scheme outputs null with one warning per restart. A whitespace-only URL counts as empty. A clip the browser can't decode (HEVC or ProRes .mov on many Windows and Linux browsers) still outputs its reference; the Video layer logs the failure.
- Loops: per index; a loop of URLs gives a loop of clips, one Video layer instance each.
- Platform and simulation: HLS (.m3u8) URLs play where the browser supports them natively (Safari, Electron with platform codecs). Headless simulation outputs the same references; the engine's simulated media clock takes Duration from the asset record.
- Coming from Origami: Origami stores the clip inside the patch. Importers put the embedded resource into the asset registry and set Video to that asset.

State per loop index: `{ ref: null, info: EMPTY, loading: false }`, where `EMPTY = { size: [0, 0], scale: 1, name: "", aspectRatio: 0 }`.

Each frame:
```
const ref = ctx.input("image");
if (!sameRef(ref, state.ref)) {
  state.ref = ref;
  if (ref === null) { state.info = EMPTY; state.loading = false; }
  else state.loading = true;                   // keep describing the previous picture until this one is ready
}
if (state.loading) {
  const m = ctx.services.mediaInfo(ref);        // the host caches and loads in the background
  if (m?.status === "ready") { state.info = describe(ref, m); state.loading = false; }
  else if (m === undefined || m.status === "error") {
    state.info = { ...EMPTY, name: nameOf(ref, m) };
    state.loading = false;
    warnOnce(ref, "imageInfo: couldn't load the picture");
  } else ctx.requestNextFrame();
}
ctx.output("naturalSize", state.info.size);
ctx.output("scale", state.info.scale);
ctx.output("name", state.info.name);
ctx.output("aspectRatio", state.info.aspectRatio);
ctx.output("loading", state.loading);

function describe(ref, m) {
  const scale = densityFromName(m.name) ?? 1;                   // "@2x" → 2, "@3x" → 3
  const size = [m.width / scale, m.height / scale];
  return { size, scale, name: nameOf(ref, m), aspectRatio: size[1] > 0 ? size[0] / size[1] : 0 };
}
```

- Name: the asset record's `name`, the chosen file's name for Photo Picker blob URLs, or a URL's last path segment, percent-decoded. The extension and any `@2x` or `@3x` suffix are removed (`hero@2x.png` → `hero`). Pictures made by patches without a file (Snapshot, Camera) give "".
- Natural Size: in points, pixel width and height divided by Scale. Photos and web pictures have Scale 1, so points equal pixels. EXIF orientation is applied, so a portrait phone photo reports a portrait size.
- Frame 0: asset pictures resolve synchronously from the registry, so frame 0 has real values. URL pictures start with Loading true and the empty values.
- Retargeting: a new picture turns Loading on that frame; the other outputs hold until its info is ready, then change together. Switching to no picture clears everything at once.
- Edge cases: no picture gives size [0, 0], scale 1, name "", aspect ratio 0, and Loading false. Zero height gives aspect ratio 0. A load failure gives the empty values with the name still derived from the reference, Loading false, and one warning per reference. Never NaN.
- Loops: per index; a loop of pictures gives loops of sizes, scales, names, and ratios.
- Muted: the empty values (scale 1) and Loading false.
- Platform and simulation: headless hosts answer from `AssetRecord.width`, `height`, and `name`; URL pictures never load headless, so they report the empty values with Loading false and one info message.
- Platform contract: `services.mediaInfo(ref)` returns `{ status, width, height, duration, name }`, cached per reference by the host. Hosts without it get the empty values and one info message per restart.
- Restart and dispose: state clears; nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's Scale description repeats Layer Info's wording; Sonobe defines Scale as the file's pixel density.

Stateless: reads the Video layer's outputs from the previous frame.

Each frame:
```
const ref = ctx.input("layer");
const read = (key) => (ref === null ? undefined : ctx.services.layerOutput(ref, key));
const t = Math.max(0, finiteOr(read("currentTime"), 0));
const d = Math.max(0, finiteOr(read("duration"), 0));
ctx.output("currentTime", t);
ctx.output("duration", d);
ctx.output("progress", d > 0 ? Math.min(t / d, 1) : 0);
ctx.output("naturalSize", validSize(read("naturalSize")) ?? [0, 0]);
```

- Timing: reads the `video` layer's `currentTime`, `duration`, and `naturalSize` outputs as resolved for the previous frame (CONVENTIONS §18.5.8). Whoever produces those outputs owns the clock: the renderer's media element in the live viewer, and the engine's simulated media clock headless (advancing with the layer's Play, Rate, Loop, Scrub, and Scrub Time, with duration and natural size from the asset record).
- Frame 0: there's no previous frame, so every output is 0 or [0, 0].
- Edge cases: an empty or missing Layer gives zeros. A layer that isn't a `video` layer gives zeros with one warning per restart. Duration 0 (still loading, or a live stream with no end) gives Progress 0. A current time past the duration gives Progress 1. Never NaN.
- Loops: per index. A reference to a looped Video layer reads `ref.instance`, or instance 0 when it has none; a loop of layer references gives one reading per index.
- Muted: zeros.
- Platform contract: `services.layerOutput(layer, key)` reads the previous frame's layer outputs, and `layerInfo(layer).type` tells whether Layer is a Video layer. Hosts without `layerOutput` get zeros and one info message per restart.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Duration and Current Time mean the same as Origami's. Origami's Layer input also accepts Video Stream and Video Keyframes layers; Sonobe reads `video` layers only.

State per instance: `{ items: [], requestId: 0, pending: null, error: false, message: "" }`. The Images and Videos outputs are whole loops, so the patch evaluates once per frame.

Each frame:
```
let picked = false;
const req = state.pending;
if (req?.result && req.id === state.requestId) {
  state.pending = null;
  if (req.result.error) { state.error = true; state.message = readable(req.result.error); }
  else if (req.result.files.length > 0) {                         // [] means canceled: change nothing
    const limit = ctx.input("multiple") ? clampInt(finiteOr(ctx.input("maxCount"), 10), 1, 100) : 1;
    const kept = req.result.files.filter(matchesMediaType).slice(0, limit);
    if (kept.length === 0) { state.error = true; state.message = "That file isn't a kind this picker accepts."; }
    else { revokeAll(state.items); state.items = kept; state.error = false; state.message = ""; picked = true; }
  }
}
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) {
  state.requestId++; state.pending = null;                        // a pending choice is ignored when it arrives
  revokeAll(state.items); state.items = []; state.error = false; state.message = "";
} else if (ctx.pulsed("open") && state.pending === null) {
  const pick = ctx.services.platform.pickMedia;
  if (pick === undefined) logOnce("photoPicker: no file picker in simulation");
  else {
    const r = { id: ++state.requestId, result: null };
    state.pending = r;
    pick({ accept: ctx.input("mediaType"), multiple: ctx.input("multiple") })
      .then((files) => { r.result = { files }; }, (error) => { r.result = { files: [], error }; });
  }
}
if (state.pending) ctx.requestNextFrame();
const first = state.items[0];
ctx.output("image", first?.image ?? null);
ctx.output("video", first?.video ?? null);
ctx.output("isVideo", first?.kind === "video");
ctx.output("naturalSize", first ? [first.width, first.height] : [0, 0]);
ctx.output("images", loop(state.items.map((i) => i.image)));
ctx.output("videos", loop(state.items.map((i) => i.video)));
ctx.output("count", state.items.length);
ctx.output("loading", state.pending !== null);
ctx.output("error", state.error);
ctx.output("errorMessage", state.message);
if (picked) ctx.pulse("picked");
```

- Pulses: `open` and `reset`. Same-frame precedence: `reset` > `open`. Open while the picker is already open is ignored, so pulses on consecutive frames open it once.
- User activation: browsers open a file picker only shortly after a real tap, click, or key press. Hosts call the picker on the frame the pulse arrives, which is inside every browser's activation window when the pulse comes from a tap. A pulse from a timer or When Prototype Starts is refused: Error turns on with the message "The picker can only open right after a tap or click."
- Items: each chosen file becomes `{ kind, image, video, width, height, name }`. A photo's `image` is a blob URL of the file with EXIF orientation applied, and `video` is null. A video's `video` is a blob URL, and `image` is a PNG still frame taken at 0.1 s (the first frame when shorter). `width` and `height` are pixels after orientation and count as points. Order is the order the platform returns, which is selection order where the system picker provides it.
- Media Type: sets the picker's accept filter (`image/*`, `video/*`, or both). Mobile pickers can still offer other kinds; files that don't match are dropped as shown.
- Max Count: applies only while Multiple is on, and extra files are dropped with one warning, because the web picker can't enforce a limit. Non-integers round down.
- Frame 0: nothing chosen: Image and Video null, Is Video false, Natural Size [0, 0], empty loops, Count 0.
- Retargeting: changing Media Type, Multiple, or Max Count affects the next Open only.
- Edge cases: canceling resolves with no files, so nothing changes, Picked doesn't fire, and Error stays as it was. A file the platform can't decode (for example HEIC in Chromium) turns Error on with a readable message and keeps the previous items. The next successful choice turns Error off.
- Loops: Images and Videos are whole loops; the other outputs describe the first item. Inputs don't loop: a looped input uses its first item and warns once per restart.
- Muted: never opens; outputs the frame 0 values.
- Platform and simulation: the desktop app uses Electron's native open dialog; browsers use a hidden `<input type="file">`, which opens the photo library or camera roll on phones. Headless simulation has no picker: Open logs `photoPicker: no file picker in simulation` once per restart and the outputs stay empty.
- Platform contract: `platform.pickMedia({ accept, multiple })` resolves with `PickedMedia` items (`[]` on cancel) and rejects with a readable error; `platform.releaseMedia` releases files the patch drops or replaces.
- Restart and dispose: forget the items, ignore pending results, and revoke every blob URL this patch created.
- Coming from Origami: Photo Library (`builtin.photolibrary`) enumerated the library with Sort By, Order, Media Type, and Album, and Photo Library Media (`builtin.photomediainfos`) unpacked one asset. Importers map Media Type, Photo Media to Images and Videos, Image, Video, Natural Size, isPlayable to Is Video, and Error. Sort By, Order, Album, Index, and isFavorite have no equivalent; importers drop them with an info diagnostic.

State per instance: `{ status: "off", facing: null, quality: null, stream: null, image: null, video: null, recorderActive: false, requests: [] }`, where `status` is `"off" | "starting" | "live" | "failed"`.

Each frame:
```
const media = ctx.services.platform.media;
const key = `${ctx.componentPath}/${ctx.id}`;
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
const facing = ctx.input("camera");
const quality = ctx.input("quality");
let captured = false, recorded = false;

// 1. Apply finished async work, dropping results of superseded requests.
for (const r of takeFinished(state.requests)) {
  if (r.superseded) continue;
  if (r.kind === "session") {
    if (r.ok) { state.status = "live"; state.stream = r.stream; }
    else { state.status = "failed"; state.stream = null; warnOnce(`camera: ${r.message}`); }
  } else if (r.kind === "capture" && r.ok) { revoke(state.image); state.image = r.image; captured = true; }
  else if (r.kind === "recording" && r.ok && r.video !== null) { revoke(state.video); state.video = r.video; recorded = true; }
}

// 2. Session.
if (media === undefined) infoOnce("camera: no camera in this host");
else if (!enabled) {
  if (state.status !== "off") { finishRecording(); supersede("session"); media.close(key); state.status = "off"; state.stream = null; }
} else if (state.status === "off" || facing !== state.facing || quality !== state.quality) {
  finishRecording();
  supersede("session");
  track("session", media.openCamera(key, { facing, quality }));   // resolves to the live stream reference
  Object.assign(state, { status: "starting", facing, quality });  // the previous stream stays on Stream meanwhile
}

// 3. Capture and record.
if (ctx.pulsed("capture")) {
  if (state.status === "live") track("capture", media.captureFrame(key));
  else warnOnce("camera: Capture needs a running camera");
}
const wantRecording = ctx.input("recording") && state.status === "live";
if (wantRecording && !state.recorderActive) { media.startRecording(key, { audio: ctx.input("recordAudio") }); state.recorderActive = true; }
if (!wantRecording && state.recorderActive) finishRecording();       // track("recording", media.stopRecording(key))

if (state.status === "starting" || state.requests.length > 0) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("stream", state.status === "live" || state.status === "starting" ? state.stream : null);
ctx.output("image", state.image);
ctx.output("video", state.video);
ctx.output("available", state.status === "live");
if (captured) ctx.pulse("captured");
if (recorded) ctx.pulse("recorded");
```

- Pulses: `capture`. Each pulse while the camera is live starts one capture, at most one per frame. Results apply in request order, so Image ends up as the newest finished photo, and Captured fires at most once per frame.
- Recording: a level input. When it turns on while the camera is starting, recording begins once the camera is live. Turning it off, disabling the camera, switching cameras, changing Quality, or restarting finishes the clip, which arrives on a later frame. Clips shorter than 0.1 s are discarded without a Recorded pulse.
- Quality: the requested frame size for Stream, photos, and recordings: Low 640 × 480, Medium 1280 × 720, High 1920 × 1080, requested as `ideal`, so the device may choose the closest size it has. Recordings use the platform's default container: WebM in Chromium and Firefox, MP4 in Safari.
- Frame 0: Enabled defaults to off, so nothing starts. With Enabled on, the session request starts on frame 0. Available is false and Stream is null until permission and hardware respond, which is never on frame 0.
- Retargeting: changing Camera or Quality reopens the session. The previous feed stays on Stream until the new one is live, and a recording in progress finishes first.
- Edge cases: denied permission, no camera, an insecure page, or a camera in use by another app sets `failed`: Available false, Stream null, and one warning with the reason. There's no retry until Enabled turns off and on, Camera or Quality changes, or the prototype restarts. Capture while the camera isn't live is ignored with one warning per restart.
- Loops: one camera per patch instance. Inputs don't loop: a looped input uses its first item and warns once per restart.
- Disabled: releases the camera so its light turns off. Stream is null and Available false; Image and Video keep the latest results.
- Muted: releases the camera; outputs are null and false; no pulses.
- Platform and simulation: live feeds use `getUserMedia({ video: { facingMode: facing === "front" ? "user" : "environment", width, height } })` without `exact`, so a computer's only camera answers either option. Stream is a video reference the renderer attaches with `srcObject`; the host names the feed with `AssetRef.live`, such as `{ live: "camera/<key>" }`. The desktop app on macOS asks the system for camera access, plus microphone access when Record Audio is on. Headless simulation has no camera: outputs stay idle deterministically, capture and record requests are logged, and one info diagnostic explains why.
- Platform contract: `platform.media` with `openCamera(key, { facing, quality })`, `close`, `captureFrame`, `startRecording`, and `stopRecording`.
- Restart and dispose: stop every track and recorder, drop pending results, revoke the blob URLs created for photos and clips, and clear Image and Video.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's quality presets (v182) aren't documented; Sonobe's Quality offers three sizes. Origami shows the feed through its Viewfinder layer; Sonobe uses a Video layer fed by Stream.

State per instance: `{ status: "off", handle: null, sound: null, recorderActive: false, requests: [] }`, where `status` is `"off" | "starting" | "live" | "failed"`.

Each frame:
```
const media = ctx.services.platform.media;
const key = `${ctx.componentPath}/${ctx.id}`;
let recorded = false;

// 1. Apply finished async work, dropping results of superseded requests.
for (const r of takeFinished(state.requests)) {
  if (r.superseded) continue;
  if (r.kind === "session") {
    if (r.ok) { state.status = "live"; state.handle = r.handle; }
    else { state.status = "failed"; state.handle = null; warnOnce(`microphone: ${r.message}`); }
  } else if (r.kind === "recording" && r.ok && r.sound !== null) { revoke(state.sound); state.sound = r.sound; recorded = true; }
}

// 2. Session.
if (media === undefined) infoOnce("microphone: no microphone in this host");
else if (!ctx.input("enabled")) {
  if (state.status !== "off") { finishRecording(); supersede("session"); media.close(key); state.status = "off"; state.handle = null; }
} else if (state.status === "off") {
  track("session", media.openMicrophone(key));              // resolves to the live metering handle
  state.status = "starting";
}

// 3. Record.
const wantRecording = ctx.input("recording") && state.status === "live";
if (wantRecording && !state.recorderActive) { media.startRecording(key, { audio: true }); state.recorderActive = true; }
if (!wantRecording && state.recorderActive) finishRecording();   // track("recording", media.stopRecording(key))

if (state.status === "starting" || state.requests.length > 0) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("sound", state.sound);
ctx.output("metering", state.status === "live" ? state.handle : null);
ctx.output("available", state.status === "live");
if (recorded) ctx.pulse("recorded");
```

- Metering handle: a sound reference naming this live input, which Audio Metering can measure. A Sound Player treats it as empty. The host names it with `AssetRef.live`, such as `{ live: "microphone/<key>" }`.
- Recording: a level input. When it turns on while the microphone is starting, recording begins once it's live. Turning it off, disabling, or restarting finishes the clip, which arrives on a later frame and replaces Sound. Clips shorter than 0.1 s are discarded without a Recorded pulse.
- Frame 0: Enabled defaults to off, so nothing starts. With Enabled on, the request starts on frame 0 and Available stays false until permission is granted.
- Edge cases: denied permission, no microphone, or an insecure page sets `failed`: Available false, Metering null, and one warning with the reason. There's no retry until Enabled turns off and on, or the prototype restarts. The platform's echo cancellation and noise suppression stay at their defaults.
- Loops: one microphone per patch instance. Inputs don't loop: a looped input uses its first item and warns once per restart.
- Disabled: releases the microphone. Metering is null and Available false; Sound keeps the latest recording.
- Muted: releases the microphone; outputs are null and false; no pulses.
- Platform and simulation: uses `getUserMedia({ audio: true })` and `MediaRecorder` with the platform's default audio container (WebM with Opus in Chromium and Firefox, MP4 with AAC in Safari). The desktop app on macOS asks the system for microphone access. Headless simulation has no microphone: outputs stay idle deterministically and one info diagnostic explains why. Automated runs never open a microphone.
- Platform contract: `platform.media` with `openMicrophone(key)`, `close`, `startRecording`, and `stopRecording`.
- Restart and dispose: stop the tracks and recorder, drop pending results, revoke blob URLs, and clear Sound.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's Audio Metering tutorial turns on both Enable and Record before levels appear; in Sonobe, metering needs only Enabled.

State per instance: `{ peaks: [] }`, a list of `{ time, db }` samples.

Each frame:
```
const bands = clampInt(finiteOr(ctx.input("resolution"), 3), 1, 128);
const percent = ctx.input("format") === "percent";
const source = ctx.input("source");
const layer = ctx.input("layer");
const meter = ctx.services.platform.audio?.meter;
const m = meter === undefined ? undefined
  : isLiveHandle(source) ? meter({ live: handleKey(source) }, bands)
  : source === null && layer !== null ? meter({ layer }, bands)
  : undefined;
let volumeDb = -160;
let bandDb = new Array(bands).fill(-160);
if (m !== undefined) {
  volumeDb = toDb(m.rms);
  bandDb = m.bands.map((db) => clamp(finiteOr(db, -160), -160, 0));
  state.peaks.push({ time: ctx.time, db: toDb(m.peak) });
  ctx.requestNextFrame();
}
state.peaks = state.peaks.filter((p) => ctx.time - p.time < 0.5);
const peakDb = state.peaks.reduce((max, p) => Math.max(max, p.db), -160);
ctx.output("volume", percent ? levelPercent(volumeDb) : volumeDb);
ctx.output("peakVolume", percent ? levelPercent(peakDb) : peakDb);
ctx.output("waveformData", loop(bandDb.map((db) => (percent ? bandPercent(db) : db))));

const toDb = (amplitude) => (amplitude > 1e-8 ? Math.min(0, 20 * Math.log10(amplitude)) : -160);
const levelPercent = (db) => clamp((db + 60) / 60, 0, 1);      // −60 dBFS and quieter → 0, full scale → 1
const bandPercent = (db) => clamp((db + 100) / 70, 0, 1);      // the AnalyserNode byte range, −100 to −30 dB
```

- Measurement (host side): an `AnalyserNode` with `fftSize` 2048 and `smoothingTimeConstant` 0.5, sampled once per frame. `rms` is the root mean square of the latest time-domain samples, and `peak` is their largest absolute sample, both 0–1. `bands` splits 20 Hz to min(20 kHz, sampleRate / 2) into Resolution log-spaced bands; each band's level is the decibel value of the mean linear power of its FFT bins, and a band narrower than one bin uses the bin holding its center frequency.
- Percent: speech close to a microphone lands around 0.3–0.6 on Volume. Decibels output the raw dBFS values in [−160, 0].
- Timing: Peak Volume is the largest per-frame peak sampled within the last 0.5 s of `ctx.time`, so it doesn't depend on frame rate.
- Frame 0: idle values: Volume and Peak Volume 0 (Percent) or −160 (Decibels), and Waveform Data with Resolution idle values.
- Precedence: Source wins when both Source and Layer are set.
- Edge cases: no Source or Layer, a Source that's a plain sound asset rather than a live handle, a Layer that isn't a `video` layer, a paused player, or no platform audio all give idle values, with one warning per restart for a plain asset or a non-video layer. Waveform Data always has Resolution items, so a bar layout doesn't collapse in silence. Non-finite Resolution uses 3; otherwise it clamps to [1, 128] and rounds down.
- Loops: Waveform Data is a whole loop, so state is per instance. Inputs don't loop: a looped input uses its first item and warns once per restart.
- Muted: idle values.
- Platform and simulation: live handles come from Sound Player and Microphone `metering` outputs (`{ live: "audio/<key>" }` and `{ live: "microphone/<key>" }`). A Video layer is tapped with `createMediaElementSource` once per media element and routed on to the speakers so the video stays audible; media from other origins without CORS headers measures silence and warns once. Headless simulation and automated runs are silent, so outputs are idle and deterministic.
- Platform contract: `platform.audio.meter(source, bands)`, where `source` is `{ live }` or `{ layer }`, returns `{ rms, peak, bands }` or undefined.
- Restart and dispose: clear the peak history. The host disconnects an analyser when the last meter on its source goes away.
- Coming from Origami: Origami describes Peak Volume's window as roughly 0.5 s; Sonobe fixes it at 0.5 s. Origami doesn't document its percent mapping; the formulas above are Sonobe's.

